title": "Will Mississippi send a Black Democrat to the US Senate? Scott Colom says don't count him out", "source": "PBS", "published": "2026-08-07T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": -11.37, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": -7.5, "poll_sd": 13.25, "poll_weight": 0.12, "fundamentals": {"margin": -6.52, "lean": -11.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": 0.1}, "rating_margin": -14.6, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.72, "baseline_margin": -10.88, "analyst_adjustment": -0.12, "key_factors": ["Mississippi's strong Republican lean (R+11) with an incumbent running", "All ten expert raters place the race at Safe or Solid R, with one Likely R", "Prediction markets price Democratic chances at only about 10 percent", "Scott Colom has raised $2.4 million and drawn outside spending, but trails in cash and polls"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a double-digit Republican win: the state leans R+11, Hyde-Smith holds incumbency advantages, and every major rater calls the race Safe or Solid R, while markets give Democrats roughly a 1-in-10 shot. Colom's fundraising and the modest recent polling gap suggest the race is not a total foregone conclusion, but nothing in the evidence supports moving the expected margin much inside ten points.", "watch": "A late surge of national Democratic spending or polling showing Colom within single digits would signal a genuine tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Cindy Hyde-Smith (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Cindy Hyde-Smith seeks reelection in a state that favors Republicans by about eleven points, and she enters the fall with the advantages of incumbency and name recognition. Every major race rater classifies the contest as Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Democrat Scott Colom roughly a ten percent chance of winning.\n\nColom, a district attorney, has run an unusually well-funded campaign for a Mississippi Democrat, raising $2.4 million and drawing outside support, and recent polling has shown a narrower gap than the state's partisan baseline. Still, he trails Hyde-Smith in cash on hand, faces a favorable national map for Republicans, and must overcome the state's deep partisan tilt. The race is a Republican favorite, with more uncertainty than a typical Safe seat but a clear GOP edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0354, "margin": -10.93}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0478, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0452, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0712, "margin": -10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0743, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0338, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0525, "margin": -11.0}]}